  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published PINN framework address inferring stochastic dynamics from noisy, irregular, or nonsteady biological data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is inference of unobserved stochastic dynamics from noisy, incomplete biological data, with emphasis on likelihood-based estimation. Two preprints target computational tractability: a frequency-domain (Fourier) route makes measurement likelihoods approximately Gaussian to accelerate Bayesian identification of reaction networks, and a latent-state maximum-likelihood method fits stochastic logistic and Lotka-Volterra diffusions to noisy, irregular microbiome time series. A third preprint estimates time-resolved E. coli division control parameters from single-cell size trajectories under nonsteady growth using maximum likelihood and AIC. The published PINN-based inverse framework extends the inverse-problem approach to size-structured fish oogenesis, inferring growth, AMH-regulated renewal and recruitment functions from cross-sectional data. What is new across these items is explicit treatment of cell-to-cell and transient variation within the inference pipeline itself.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-12"
}
